  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nOur persistent upper-level ridge and surface high pressure is \nexpected to retrograde westward back over the Central Plains \nover the next 48 hours, keeping us in high heat through Labor \nDay. The current Heat Advisory remains in effect through \nSaturday evening, and has been expanded northwestward slightly. \nThe hottest locations in our CWA will be the southeastern \ncounties, where afternoon and early evening heat indices will \nreach up to ~107 F, with 12z HREF indicating 60 - 80% chances \nfor heat indices to top 105 F. Overnight lows in the Heat \nAdvisory zone will offer little relief only getting down to the \nlow- to-mid 70s F. Heat indices even for our far northwestern \ncounties in greater central Nebraska will reach into the upper \n90s, and these portions of the CWA still only have 10 - 20% \nchances for overnight lows to bottom out ~65 F. \n\nDry conditions are favored through tomorrow (FRI) evening. \nFriday evening ~5:00 PM, a few isolated thunderstorms will be \npossible (12z HREF probabilities 10 - 20% chances), primarily \nwest of Highway 183 and north of Kearney. A couple storms also \nlook to be possible for our counties along the KS / NE state \nline as southeasterly surface flow with relatively higher \ndewpoints and a strengthening 850-hPa jet overlap as the ridge /\nsurface high pushes back west. The 12z RRFS and ARW appear the \nmost aggressive with these storms. However, confidence is lower \n(10%) in these storms, but it will be worth watching. The best \nchances for precip do look to hold west of North Platte's CWA, \nbut a heaviest-precip scenario (i.e. 95th percentile totals) \nwould bring very localized totals of 0.5 - 0.75 in. Highest \nchances to realize these totals would be west of Highway 183. \nRegardless, the likeliest scenario is that the majority stay \ndry, and those that do receive rain get nothing more than 0.1 \nin. \n\nSurface winds today will continue at 10 - 20 mph, wobbling from\nout of the SSW to SSE. Gusts today will be highest for those in\nthe Heat Advisory zone, offering a slight bit of relief. \nToday's gusts will top out near 25 mph. Tonight, a few of our \nnorthwestern counties can see a brief shift to northerly winds, \nbut southerly flow will be the majority's wind direction through\ntonight. There is some uncertainty as to how strong tonight's \ngust can get in the Heat Advisory zone, as a surface inversion \nis likely to develop but low-level winds will be strong. Some \nmixing would allow relatively breezier conditions to continue \novernight in the Heat Advisory zone. Tomorrow (FRI) afternoon, a\nshift to more southeasterly flow at 10 - 15 mph will help bring\nin slightly higher moisture content for our potential storms. \nBeyond FRI, calmer south-southeasterly flow is expected for SAT \nand SUN. MON, however, looks to be the windiest day upcoming, \nwith 15 - 25 mph sustained winds and 13z NBM probabilities of \ngusts >35 mph reaching ~60%.",
